one thing's very clear in this data and  that is firms appreciate two things  number one they appreciate people who  have high ability shall we say people  who get good grades good test scores  other measures of ability the other  thing they appreciate is that you've had  some experience in their industry either  because you learned something or the  thing we're focusing on is that you've  reduced the uncertainty as to whether or  not you'd be a good fit in their  industry if you've never worked in the  industry that I'm hiring you to work in  before we don't know how well you're  gonna fit there so when I got hired into  academia they already knew I would fit  that pretty well because I'd been a grad  student and I'd been in school a long  time but they didn't know it for sure  and so over a period of time you figure  out hey this person can do research and  can teach and things like that it's that  type of thing and the more we see that  you've had experience doing that the  better sense we have that you will fit  in our industry let's consider two  applicants for a job they're both second  year MBA is applying for full-time jobs  at the same company holding everything  else equal if one of those candidates  has worked in that industry before their  odds of getting that job are more than  double that of the person who's never  worked in that industry in fact there  are about 2.4 times as high so just  taking you know as a baseline suppose  there's a company which accepts 10% of  its applicants that have never worked in  the industry before that same company  would accept a would make offers to  about 24% of the people who have worked  in that industry before so you can see  that's quite a dramatic difference it's  more than twice as likely to get the job  there's a very natural rent-to-own if  you will arrangement for MBAs which is  called the summer internship and it's  extremely useful for testing the waters  so an MBA who says hmm I'd really like  to work in investment banking but  they've never really worked in banking  they can by going to work in banking  over the summer they solve two problems  one they find out whether or not they  like work in banking and the other is a  bank might be very reluctant to hire  somebody who's never worked in  investment banking if they're gonna have  to go through a lot of effort to fire  them they fire them naturally at the end  of a summer internship there's a nice  natural breaking point in the employment  relationship now a lot of those people  get offers to come back and that's very  helpful in terms of making the market  work smoothly but if it doesn't work out  for some reason the firm isn't stuck  with a person that doesn't want which  can be very disruptive and expensive you  see that same thing where certainty with  where knowing that the person has  experience is valuable to that employer  but it's not quite as valuable because  they're more willing to take a chance on  the outsider because at the end of the  summer they can get rid of them for free  so in that in in the example there if we  have two applicants to a similar firm  for summer internships the the person  with experience in that industry is  about 1.8 times as likely to get the job  offer  whereas if it were a full-time job it's  well over 2 times closer to 2.4 times  we see a lot of people who get summer  internships get an offer to go back and  work there in our data it's about half  now a lot of the people who don't get  offers to come back that's for budgetary  reasons they go to a small company that  turns out not to have the ability to  hire the person back or something like  that but a lot of it if you look at  companies that have large numbers of  summer interns they're making offers to  maybe most of them but far from all of  them so firms and students are able to  take advantage of what they learn about  each other over the summer to get to  better matches the summer is very useful  both for learning about firms learning  about industries learning about each  other and so forth we see a lot of firms  make offers to the people that work  there over the summer we see a lot of  students go back to those firms but a  lot choose dot too so both sides of the  market are learning something both about  the mats they have with that firm and  how that compares to their outside  options that they then can go compare  that they can then go look at over the  next recruiting season the time to take  a chances in the summer people will take  a chance on you and your summer  internships much more than they will in  full-time jobs and you see more more at  least Stanford students I don't know  about other business schools as much  taking trying to split their time  between two internships over the summer  and especially if you want to try  something new that's a great idea as  long as each of those internships is  long enough for you to learn whether or  not this is the right place for you so  if a five-week internship is just enough  time to you know learn where your desk  is and you don't really get a great feel  for the industry then that's not gonna  solve the problem but if five weeks or  six weeks or Everlong a split internship  is is long enough to really learn about  that industry in that business then  splitting your summer up because it  gives you so many more options going  forward and so much more information  going forward
 

